      "name": "InitializeGameObject",
      "signature": "void * InitializeGameObject(void * pGameObject)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "void *",
      "comment": "Initializes a game object instance with virtual function table and critical section synchronization.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Save current FS:[0] exception handler pointer to stack for restoration\n2. Install SEH frame with custom exception handler (SEH_ExceptionHandlerGameObjectInit)\n3. Load object pointer from stack parameter (ESP+0x14)\n4. Call InitializeObjectBase to perform object-specific field initialization\n5. Set exception state to 0 (success) in local variable\n6. Store virtual function table pointer (g_pVFTableGameObject) at object offset +0x0\n7. Calculate critical section address at object offset +0x50\n8. Call InitializeCriticalSection to prepare synchronization primitive\n9. Set exception state to -1 for SEH cleanup unwind\n10. Restore previous exception handler from stack\n11. Pop ESI and restore stack, return object pointer with callee cleanup (RET 0x4)\n\nParameters:\npGameObject (void*): Pointer to uninitialized game object instance. Must point to at least 96 bytes\nof writable memory allocated for InitializedGameObject structure. Critical section storage is\nrequired at offset +0x50 (24 bytes minimum).\n\nReturns:\nvoid*: Returns same pGameObject pointer passed as parameter, enabling call chaining\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- SEH frame enables exception handling during initialization\n- Virtual function table at offset +0x0 is used for virtual method dispatch\n- Critical section at offset +0x50 must be exactly 24 bytes (CRITICAL_SECTION size)\n- Exception state tracking (-1 before/after init, 0 during) indicates success/failure\n- __stdcall calling convention means caller must clean stack (RET 0x4 cleans 4 bytes)\n- FS:[0] exception handler chain allows nested SEH frames in call stack",
      "name_source": "LoD/1.11",
      "method": "CAL",
      "index": "CAL:354769970a101ec5c1bbc763cc307761",
