      "comment": "Destroys a game object instance and optionally deallocates its memory.\n\nThis function is responsible for the complete lifecycle destruction of a game\nobject, including cleanup of internal members and optional memory deallocation.\nIt serves as a destructor wrapper that coordinates object cleanup with memory\nmanagement.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Save callee-preserved register ESI\n2. Move implicit this pointer (ECX) to ESI for safe preservation across calls\n3. Call DestroyGameObjectWithMembers to recursively destroy nested object members\n4. Test bit 0 of bFreeMemory flag via [ESP+0x8]\n5. If flag bit is clear (not set), jump to cleanup_and_return\n6. Test ESI pointer for null validity\n7. If pointer is null, jump to cleanup_and_return\n8. Push 0x0 and -0x1 as additional parameters for deallocation\n9. Push szDeleteCallbackObjectName string reference (0x6fc36838)\n10. Call DeallocateMemoryBlock with (object pointer, callback string, -0x1, 0x0)\n11. Load ESI into EAX as return value (always return object pointer)\n12. Restore ESI register\n13. Return with 0x4 byte stack cleanup (caller pops this and bFreeMemory)\n\nParameters:\n- this (ECX, implicit): Pointer to the game object instance being destroyed\n- bFreeMemory (Stack[0x8], byte): Flag controlling memory deallocation behavior\n  Bit 0 = 1: Deallocate object memory after member destruction\n  Bit 0 = 0: Only destroy members, do not deallocate memory\n\nReturns:\n- EAX (void*): Always returns the input object pointer after destruction\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- If bFreeMemory bit 0 is not set: Only DestroyGameObjectWithMembers is called,\n  memory deallocation is completely skipped\n- If object pointer is null: DestroyGameObjectWithMembers is still called (may\n  handle null gracefully), but memory deallocation is skipped\n- Return value is always the input pointer regardless of deallocation status,\n  allowing caller to validate destruction\n\nMemory Access Patterns:\n- [ESP+0x8]: bFreeMemory flag (tested with TEST and JZ instructions)\n- ESI register: Holds object pointer throughout function (implicit this from ECX)\n- 0x6fc36838: Points to \"delete\" string constant used as callback identifier",
      "name_source": "LoD/1.11",
