      "comment": "Multiplies a large fixed-point number by an integer multiplier.\\n\\nAlgorithm:\\n1. Load and initialize security guard using XOR with frame pointer\\n2. Check if multiplier is zero - skip processing if true\\n3. Handle sign: negate multiplier if negative, select alternate table base\\n4. Check if nBitWidth is 0 - zero result if true\\n5. Loop through multiplier in 8-bit chunks:\\n   a. Shift multiplier right by 3 bits (divide by 8) to get next chunk\\n   b. Extract remainder (multiplier & 7) for table lookup\\n   c. Add 0x54 to table base for data section offset\\n   d. Calculate entry address from remainder * 0xc (12 bytes per entry)\\n   e. Check entry validity by testing bit 15 (0x8000) - skip if invalid\\n   f. Copy three DWORDs from table entry to temp buffer\\n   g. Call MultiplyLargeNumbers to multiply and accumulate result\\n6. Validate security guard XOR before return\\n7. Return to caller\\n\\nParameters:\\n- pResult: Pointer to result buffer (stores accumulated product)\\n- multiplier: Integer multiplier (positive or negative)\\n- nBitWidth: Bit width indicator; negative values select alternate table\\n\\nReturns:\\nvoid - Result stored directly in pResult buffer\\n\\nSpecial Cases:\\n- If nBitWidth == 0: pResult zeroed to 0\\n- If multiplier == 0: processing skipped entirely\\n- Negative multiplier uses g_MultiplyTableNegative (0x6fc422a0)\\n- Positive multiplier uses g_MultiplyTablePositive (0x6fc42140)\\n- Table entries marked with 0x8000 flag are skipped during processing\\n- Security guard uses XOR with frame pointer for stack corruption detection\\n\\nStructure Layout:\\nEach table entry is 0xc (12) bytes containing three DWORDs:\\n  Offset 0x0: First coefficient (lower 2 bytes + upper 2 bytes split)\\n  Offset 0x4: Second coefficient (word at offset 0x4)\\n  Offset 0x8: Third coefficient (dword at offset 0x8)\\nEntries are accessed as: tableBase + 0x54 + (remainder * 0xc)",
